## Stack: Swift / SwiftUI

- Open the project in Xcode (`open *.xcodeproj` or `*.xcworkspace`), or build
  from the command line with `xcodebuild`.
- Build: `xcodebuild build -scheme <Scheme> -destination 'generic/platform=iOS'`.
- Test: `xcodebuild test -scheme <Scheme> -destination 'platform=iOS Simulator,OS=latest,name=<iPhone>'`,
  on the newest iPhone simulator you have (CI picks one by itself).
- Lint: `swiftlint` (and `swiftlint --fix` to auto-correct). Run it before every
  commit; the CI lint job must stay clean.
- **Always the latest:** the latest stable Xcode and its SDKs, the latest Swift
  language mode (`swift-tools-version`, `swiftLanguageModes`, `SWIFT_VERSION`),
  and the latest iOS major as the deployment target. Docs and CI say "the
  latest" and never name a version, so a new release never leaves them stale.
- SwiftUI first; value types (struct/enum) by default, a class only for reference
  semantics or framework needs. One primary view per file, named for what it shows.
- Keep views declarative: business logic lives in observable view models and in
  engines/services, not in the view body. Dispatch heavy work off the main actor
  and publish results back on `@MainActor`.
- Use Swift Concurrency (`async`/`await`, actors) over completion handlers or raw
  `DispatchQueue`; never silence a data-race warning to ship.
- Names follow the Swift API Design Guidelines (`UpperCamelCase` types,
  `lowerCamelCase` members; no `NS`/`k` prefixes on new symbols).
- Doc comments are DocC `///`: a one-line summary, a blank `///` line, then
  `- Parameters:` and `- Returns:` where they apply. Group members under
  `// MARK: -` dividers.
- Dependencies via Swift Package Manager; commit `Package.resolved`. No CocoaPods
  or Carthage in new repos. Never commit signing assets, API keys, or `.env`.
